<article class="env-lemma" id="051W">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/051W">Lemma <span data-tag="051W">10.118.4</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $0 \to M_1 \to M_2 \to M_3 \to 0$ be a short exact sequence of $S$-modules. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  U(R \to S, M_1) \cap U(R \to S, M_3) \subset U(R \to S, M_2).  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $u \in U(R \to S, M_1) \cap U(R \to S, M_3)$. Choose $f_1, f_3 \in R$ such that $u \in D(f_1)$, $u \in D(f_3)$ and such that (<a href="/tag/051U" data-tag="051U">10.118.3.1</a>) holds for $f_1$ and $M_1$ and for $f_3$ and $M_3$. Then set $f = f_1f_3$. Then $u \in D(f)$ and (<a href="/tag/051U" data-tag="051U">10.118.3.1</a>) holds for $f$ and both $M_1$ and $M_3$. An extension of free modules is free, and an extension of finitely presented modules is finitely presented (Lemma <a href="/tag/0519" data-tag="0519">10.5.3</a>). Hence we see that (<a href="/tag/051U" data-tag="051U">10.118.3.1</a>) holds for $f$ and $M_2$. Thus $u \in U(R \to S, M_2)$ and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>